//
// The two refusals the `&`-capture fix KEEPS (loft#1276).  Its passing twin is
// `1276-a-closure-captures-a-ref-parameter.loft`; these shapes cannot live there because a
// file that must compile cannot also carry the ones that must not.
//
// 1. A `&` SCALAR written from inside a closure.  The capture holds a COPY of the caller's
//    value — `(L-CapScalar)` — so there is no shared record for the write to land in, and
//    before this refusal existed the program compiled and answered quietly wrong: `bump(n)`
//    on `n = 5` gave 15 where 16 is correct, the closure's increment dropped through a
//    parameter whose whole purpose is the write-back.  Refusing is the point, and it is a
//    DECISION: doc/claude/DESIGN_DECISIONS.md C115 records why no code change closes it —
//    `(L-CapScalar)` gives the closure a copy, so there is no shared record to write into.
//
// 2. A `&` parameter that is genuinely only READ.  Widening the write-walk to credit a
//    lambda's writes must not credit a lambda's READS as well — this cell is what says the
//    lint that was right is still right.
//
// @falsified-at: 9d571f60 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/3 -> 3/3,
//   native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/3 -> 3/3.
//
//   On the control the read-only cells are refused for the RIGHT reason and the scalar-write
//   cell is refused for four WRONG ones, so the expectation set as a whole fails there; here
//   all three match.  Negative control run by hand: replacing the first expectation's text
//   with a string nothing emits turns the file red, which is what says the cell is load-
//   bearing rather than merely present.
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal are equal on both trees. Falsify prints only
//   the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both.
//   The native side was not scored in this sweep.

// ⚠ This declaration is load-bearing for the HARNESS, not for the test.  An `@EXPECT_ERROR`
// before the first `fn`/`struct`/`enum` binds FILE-level rather than to the function under
// it, and a file-level expectation is checked but not named in the run's report — so the
// first cell read as *"0 expected errors"* while still being enforced.  A green line that
// under-reports what it checked is the shape a later reader deletes as dead.
struct Anchor1276 { unused: integer }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot write to the `&` parameter 'p' from a closure
fn pe_1276_scalar_write_from_a_closure(p: &integer) {
  g = fn() { p += 1; };
  g();
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: has & but is never modified
fn pe_1276_read_only_ref_is_still_reported(q: &integer) -> integer { q + 0 }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: has & but is never modified
fn pe_1276_read_only_ref_read_from_a_closure(r: &integer) -> integer {
  g = fn() -> integer { r + 0 };
  g()
}
